= Knowledge market =

A '''knowledge market''' is an arrangement that matches people or organizations seeking knowledge with people, organizations, or resources able to supply it. Exchange may involve money, intellectual-property rights, reputation, reciprocal assistance, access, career benefits, recognition, or other incentives. Knowledge markets can operate inside organizations, between firms, through professional networks, or on digital platforms.

The market metaphor is useful because knowledge does not always flow freely even when people are connected. Potential buyers must discover who knows something, suppliers need incentives to share, quality must be assessed, and intermediaries may be needed to match participants. In organizational knowledge-management literature, knowledge markets are commonly described in terms of '''buyers''', '''sellers''', and '''brokers''', with reciprocity and reputation often functioning as non-monetary currencies.

Knowledge markets differ from ordinary commodity markets because knowledge can often be copied without depriving the original holder, its value can be difficult to judge before disclosure, and transfer may depend on tacit skill or context.

== Historical and theoretical background ==

Friedrich Hayek's “The Use of Knowledge in Society” emphasized that economically relevant knowledge is dispersed among individuals and that coordination mechanisms must make use of local knowledge that no central planner fully possesses.<ref name="hayek">[https://www.econlib.org/library/Essays/hykKnw.html Hayek, F. A. (1945), “The Use of Knowledge in Society,” ''American Economic Review'' 35(4), 519–530.]</ref>

Kenneth Arrow's work on invention and information economics highlighted distinctive properties of information: producing new knowledge can be costly, copying or using it can be comparatively cheap, and disclosure may undermine the ability to charge for it.<ref name="arrow">[https://www.nber.org/books-and-chapters/rate-and-direction-inventive-activity-economic-and-social-factors/economic-welfare-and-allocation-resources-invention Arrow, K. J. (1962), “Economic Welfare and the Allocation of Resources for Invention.”]</ref>

Knowledge-management writers later used explicit marketplace language for internal organizational exchange. Davenport and Prusak described knowledge buyers, sellers, brokers, and price systems in which reciprocity, reputation, and altruism can matter even when money is absent.

Policy research uses the broader term '''knowledge networks and markets''' for mechanisms and institutions supporting creation, exchange, dissemination, and use of knowledge and knowledge-based assets.<ref name="oecd">[https://doi.org/10.1787/5k44wzw9q5zv-en OECD (2013), ''Knowledge Networks and Markets'', OECD Science, Technology and Industry Policy Papers.]</ref>

== What is exchanged? ==

A knowledge market can exchange several different objects or services:

* expert advice;
* answers to questions;
* research reports;
* datasets;
* patents or licenses;
* software;
* designs;
* methods and procedures;
* training;
* problem-solving effort;
* access to specialized networks;
* knowledge embodied in skilled labor.

These are economically different.

A patent license transfers specified legal rights. Consulting sells time and expertise. A public research paper may be freely accessible. A hiring market transfers access to a person's future labor rather than ownership of their knowledge.

The term “knowledge market” should therefore identify what is actually exchanged.

== Buyers, sellers and brokers ==

A '''buyer''' has a problem, question, or need.

A '''seller''' possesses or can produce relevant knowledge.

A '''broker''' helps participants find one another, interpret needs, evaluate expertise, or structure the exchange.

Brokers can be people, platforms, technology-transfer offices, search systems, professional associations, or other intermediaries.

Market performance depends on whether participants can discover the right counterpart at reasonable cost.

== Prices and non-monetary currencies ==

External knowledge markets can use ordinary prices: consulting fees, subscriptions, royalties, bounties, or licensing payments.

Internal organizational markets often operate without cash. Exchange can be motivated by:

* reciprocity;
* reputation;
* promotion;
* status;
* professional identity;
* obligation;
* altruism;
* shared mission.

This means a “price” in an internal knowledge market is often metaphorical or multidimensional.

Extrinsic rewards can also crowd out intrinsic motivation. Paying people for every answer can reduce voluntary cooperation or encourage low-quality contributions.<ref name="serrat">[https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-981-10-0983-9_110 Serrat, O. (2017), “On Internal Knowledge Markets.”]</ref>

== Search and matching ==

A major function of knowledge markets is reducing search cost.

Someone with a specialized problem may not know:

* whether relevant knowledge exists;
* who possesses it;
* how to describe the problem;
* what a reasonable price is;
* how to evaluate the answer.

Directories, reputation systems, search engines, expert networks, tags, and brokers reduce these frictions.

The best expert is not always the best match. Availability, communication ability, domain fit, confidentiality, and cost also matter.

== Information asymmetry ==

Knowledge markets have an unusual quality problem: a buyer may not know whether the seller's answer is valuable until after receiving enough information to evaluate it.

This creates a disclosure dilemma. If the seller reveals the knowledge fully before payment, the buyer may no longer need to purchase it. If the seller reveals nothing, the buyer cannot evaluate quality.

Mechanisms used to reduce this problem include:

* credentials;
* samples;
* reputation;
* warranties;
* staged disclosure;
* escrow;
* independent review;
* repeat relationships;
* outcome-contingent payment.

None is perfect.

== Non-rivalry and excludability ==

Many knowledge goods are '''non-rival''': one person's use does not necessarily reduce another person's ability to use the same knowledge.

Excludability varies. Trade secrets can be protected through secrecy and contracts. Patents create legal exclusion rights for inventions. Copyright controls some forms of expression. Public-domain knowledge can be difficult or undesirable to exclude.

These properties make some knowledge resemble a [[Public good]], but not all knowledge is a pure public good. Tacit expertise, scarce expert time, confidential information, and legally restricted assets can remain rival or excludable in important ways.

== Internal knowledge markets ==

Organizations use internal knowledge markets to improve transfer across departments or locations.

A pull-style system can allow employees to post questions while experts choose whether to answer. Payment can be monetary, virtual, reputational, or organizational.

Research on internal knowledge markets shows that design must align incentives, information technology, and heterogeneous expertise.<ref name="internal-design">[https://doi.org/10.1108/02635571211255048 “Knowledge market in organizations: incentive alignment and IT support,” ''Industrial Management & Data Systems'' 112(7), 2012.]</ref>

Internal markets can fail because of weak incentives, poor trust, lack of time, knowledge hoarding, uncertain quality, or the mismatch between market mechanisms and the nature of the knowledge being exchanged.<ref name="failure">[https://doi.org/10.1016/j.im.2006.09.001 Brydon, M. and Vining, A. R. (2006), “Understanding the failure of internal knowledge markets,” ''Information & Management'' 43(8), 964–974.]</ref>

== External markets ==

External knowledge markets include:

* consulting;
* expert networks;
* paid question-answering;
* research services;
* patent licensing;
* technology transfer;
* challenge and prize platforms;
* data marketplaces;
* specialized training.

A platform can subsidize one side of the market while charging another. For example, question askers may use a service for free while experts are compensated through advertising, subscriptions, or institutional funding.

== Open and commons-based alternatives ==

Not all knowledge exchange should be organized through prices.

Open-source software, open science, Wikipedia-like collaboration, public repositories, and professional communities use combinations of reputation, reciprocity, norms, public funding, and shared infrastructure.

These systems can still be analyzed as exchange mechanisms, but calling every exchange a market can obscure the differences between markets, commons, communities, and public institutions.

== Knowledge markets versus prediction markets ==

A '''knowledge market''' matches demand for knowledge with supply of expertise or knowledge assets.

A '''prediction market''' trades contracts whose prices aggregate beliefs about uncertain future events.

Prediction markets can produce information, but they are a specific forecasting mechanism rather than a synonym for knowledge markets.

== Knowledge markets versus knowledge ecosystems ==

A [[Knowledge ecosystem]] includes broader processes of creation, validation, preservation, governance, and application.

A knowledge market focuses specifically on exchange and matching.

A market can exist inside a knowledge ecosystem, while the ecosystem also contains non-market institutions such as peer review, repositories, regulation, and education.

== Quality and epistemic validation ==

Market success does not prove truth.

A highly rated answer can be wrong. A popular consultant can give biased advice. A patent can be legally valid without being commercially useful. A widely purchased report can contain unsupported claims.

Knowledge markets therefore need epistemic mechanisms separate from price:

* evidence requirements;
* peer review;
* outcome evaluation;
* reproducibility;
* conflict-of-interest disclosure;
* provenance;
* correction and refund mechanisms.

Reputation helps only when it correlates with real performance and is resistant to manipulation.

== Incentives and strategic behavior ==

Participants can game knowledge markets.

Possible strategies include:

* overstating expertise;
* withholding information to raise price;
* copying others' answers;
* colluding on reputation;
* posting trivial contributions for rewards;
* monopolizing brokerage;
* strategically delaying disclosure.

Mechanism design must account for these incentives rather than assuming participants truthfully reveal knowledge and quality.

== Access and fairness ==

Markets allocate according to willingness and ability to pay unless designed otherwise.

This can create unequal access to scientific, legal, technical, or medical expertise. Public funding, subsidies, open-access policies, libraries, and professional obligations can correct some access failures.

A market that efficiently matches wealthy buyers with experts can still be socially undesirable if critical knowledge remains unavailable to others.

== Measurement ==

A knowledge market should be evaluated against its stated goal.

Possible metrics include:

* time to match;
* answer quality;
* resolution rate;
* buyer satisfaction;
* repeat use;
* verification accuracy;
* participation diversity;
* concentration of sellers;
* price dispersion;
* access inequality;
* correction rate.

Popularity and transaction volume alone are weak proxies for knowledge quality.

== Artificial intelligence and knowledge markets ==

AI systems can alter knowledge markets by reducing the cost of producing answers, searching repositories, translating expertise, and matching questions to sources.

This creates new design questions:

* Is an answer produced by a human expert, a model, or both?
* What provenance is available?
* Who is liable for errors?
* How are copied model outputs detected?
* Does cheap generated supply crowd out expert participation?
* Are many “independent” answers generated by the same underlying model?

Markets should distinguish provenance and validation rather than treating all answers as homogeneous commodities.

